/-!
# Digital roots in an arbitrary base

The digital root of `n` in base `b` is the single digit reached by repeatedly summing the
base-`b` digits of `n`.  Closed form: `0` at `0`, and `1 + (n - 1) % (b - 1)` otherwise.

Everything in this file is stated for a general base `b` and a general divisor `d` of `b - 1`;
the decimal specialisations (`b = 10`, `d = 3`, the trinity `{3, 6, 9}`) are in
`RequestProject.DigitalRoot.Decimal`.

Main results:

* `digitalRootBase_digitsSum` — casting out `(b-1)`s: summing the base-`b` digits does not
  change the base-`b` digital root;
* `dvd_digitalRootBase_iff` — for `d ∣ b - 1`, the digital root detects divisibility by `d`;
* `mem_trinityBase_iff` — membership in the base-`b` trinity of `d` is divisibility by `d`;
* `card_trinityBase`, `exists_trinity_card_three_iff` — the trinity of `d` has `(b-1)/d`
  elements, so a three-element trinity exists exactly when `3 ∣ b - 1`;
* `digitalRootBase_add`, `digitalRootBase_mul` — digital roots may be taken before adding or
  multiplying;
* `digitalRootBase_pow_period`, `digitalRootBase_pow_notMem_trinityBase` — the general
  "doubling circuit": periodicity of the digital roots of the powers of `a`, and the reason
  those powers miss the trinity of `d` when `a` is coprime to `d`.
-/

