comments.text:
/-!
# Translations as homomorphisms of Markov classes

A *translation* of braid words is any map `T : (Σ n, Word n) → (Σ n, Word n)` — for example an
encoding of a text in another script, in another reading direction, or with another sign
convention.  The question the project asks of every such map is whether it respects the closed
link, i.e. Markov equivalence.  This file proves:

* `MarkovTranslation` — `T` sends every single Markov move to a Markov equivalence;
* `MarkovTranslation.map` — such a `T` sends Markov-equivalent words to Markov-equivalent
  words, so it induces a map on Markov classes (closed links);
* `MarkovTranslation.invariant_comp` — composing any Markov invariant with `T` gives a Markov
  invariant;
* `MarkovTranslation.comp` — translations compose;
* `mirrorT_markovTranslation` — changing every crossing sign is a translation;
* `reverseT_markovTranslation` — reading every word backwards is a translation (the
  stabilisation case needs a cyclic rotation, done with one braid move and one conjugation);
* `flipT_markovTranslation` — seeing the page from behind (`σᵢ ↦ σ_{n-2-i}`) is a translation:
  the flip is conjugation by the half twist `Δ` (`flipHom_eq_conj_delta`), so every word is
  Markov equivalent to its own flip (`markovEquiv_flipT_self`);
* `pageSymmetry_markovTranslation` — hence all eight composites of flip, reverse and mirror are
  translations;
* `Bible.convert_reverse` and `Bible.markovEquiv_convert_reverse` — the Bible-braid encoder
  intertwines reading a text backwards with reversing its braid word, so texts with
  Markov-equivalent braids still have Markov-equivalent braids when both are read backwards.
-/

