comments.text:
/-!
# Hecke eigenvalue systems

For a normalised Hecke eigenform of weight `k`, the eigenvalues `a n` of the Hecke operators
`T n` satisfy

* `a 1 = 1`,
* `a (m * n) = a m * a n` whenever `gcd m n = 1`,
* `a (p ^ (j + 2)) = a p * a (p ^ (j + 1)) - p ^ (k - 1) * a (p ^ j)` for every prime `p`.

`HeckeSystem` axiomatises exactly this data — no analysis, just the multiplicative structure —
over an arbitrary commutative ring.  Everything below is a consequence of those three axioms.

* `eq_on_prime_pow` — two systems agreeing at `p` agree at every power of `p`;
* `eq_of_primeFactors_subset`, `eq_on_Smooth` — agreement at the primes of `S` forces
  agreement at every positive `S`-smooth integer;
* `eq_on_smooth_two_three` — the case `S = {2, 3}`: the two eigenvalues `a 2` and `a 3`
  determine `a n` for every `3`-smooth `n = 2 ^ i * 3 ^ j`;
* `eq_of_eq_on_primes` — agreement at all primes is agreement at every positive integer;
* `eq_on_le_of_eq_on_primes_le` — agreement at the primes `p ≤ N` already forces agreement at
  every `n ≤ N`.  This is the honest, purely algebraic shadow of multiplicity one; the genuine
  multiplicity-one and Sturm-bound theorems are analytic and are not proved here;
* `exists_separating_bound` — a pigeonhole separation bound for a finite family of systems;
* `a_zero_undetermined` — the axioms say nothing about `a 0`, so the hypotheses `n ≠ 0` above
  cannot be dropped;
* `a_four`, `a_six`, `a_nine`, `a_twelve`, `a_twentyseven` — the explicit low-degree formulas;
* `heckeSystem_cardDivisors` — the axioms are not vacuous: `n ↦ d(n)` is a Hecke system.
-/

